WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:07.399
Oh great, you are back! What a relief, I trust that you were responsible

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:12.080
right? You know masks, six-feet, treat everything you touch as if it's raw chicken?

00:00:12.809 --> 00:00:17.749
Yeah, it's pretty scary out there though, huh? How do we even get to this point?

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:20.690
It seems like it was just a few weeks ago, when we didn't even know to take this whole thing

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:21.260
serious-

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:25.940
Oh. It's cuz it HAS been only a few weeks?

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:26.980
Right.

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:31.660
Well, listen, there are dozens of reasons why this is snowballed into a global pandemic,

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:33.840
but I'm not really qualified to talk about most of it.

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:37.540
But there is something I could talk about, if you're up for it!

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:43.730
It's exponential growth. It is really really hard for human beings to understand exponential growth

00:00:43.829 --> 00:00:49.518
Most people don't understand what an exponential is. When you say something is expanding exponentially...

00:00:49.649 --> 00:00:52.788
So you take 100 you get 1,000 and get 10,000

00:00:52.788 --> 00:00:56.200
Our intuition isn't really capable of understanding exponential growth,

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:59.260
and the irony is that it's actually fairly easy to understand on paper!

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:01.000
It's not hard math!

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:05.740
I mean, it's no vector calculus or differential equations or taxes.

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:12.900
It's just raising a number like 2 to the power of another number, you know squared, cubed, 2 to the power of 4.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:19.220
We rarely have need for exponents larger than this. So what if we raise 2 to the power of 100?

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:23.119
Here is a classic sheet of paper. Nothing remarkable about it.

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:25.780
If I was to double this, I'd have two sheets of paper.

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:26.640
If I was to double this again,

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:30.460
I'd have four. Again, and I'd have eight so on and so forth, you get the idea.

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:33.000
So what if I doubled this a hundred times?

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:36.400
Don't calculate the answer!

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:37.780
What does your gut tell you?

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:39.500
We go straight from the gut!

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:44.220
Old-school Corridor Crew fans might recognize when I asked my coworkers this exact question.

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:50.320
I don't know, probably like 3 of these buildings.

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:51.560
200 feet?

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:53.260
Ok.

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:57.520
Probably like, freakin' the distance of the state of Texas or something, something outrageous.

00:01:57.900 --> 00:01:59.980
That is pretty dang outrageous.

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:03.440
500 meters?

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:04.420
500 meters

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:06.020
Yeah, okay. Here's a hint:

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:08.820
How far into outer space will it go?

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:09.880
Oh my god.

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:11.340
At 10 doublings,

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:16.080
the stack of paper would be 10 centimeters tall, but just 5 doublings later

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:18.820
you wouldn't be able to stack it in your house!

00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:19.880
At 20 doublings,

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:23.400
the stack of paper would stretch across an entire soccer pitch

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:24.360
At 30 doublings,

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:26.920
it would officially reach outer space.

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:29.900
(Stretching over a 100km straight up)

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:30.740
To get to the moon,

00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:35.640
we would just need 12 more doublings on top of that. So what about a hundred doublings?

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:37.780
I bet you're prepared for a pretty large number,

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:39.660
But trust me, you're really not.

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:41.600
This stack would be so tall,

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:44.140
the only way to measure would be in light-years.

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:47.440
13 billion light-years.

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:49.700
That's over 500 meters man.

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:54.660
The thing about exponential growth, is that most of the growth happens at the last moment.

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:57.720
It's what gives graphs like this, that hockey-stick look.

00:02:57.720 --> 00:02:59.140
And in the case of doublings,

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:02.680
literally half of the total amount was gained at the final step,

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:04.000
no matter which step you're on.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:08.100
In fact at this virus's current rate of spread in the next four months,

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:11.900
it will have infected one trillion people.

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:12.540
Wait a minute...

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:13.810
Okay, okay

00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:15.810
So technically the spread of this virus-

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:18.800
-actually follows what's called a logistic curve.

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:21.760
As more people use social distancing or... die,

00:03:21.820 --> 00:03:26.280
There are fewer people the virus can infect and the rate of infection begins to decrease

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:29.580
This is called the inflection point and is the peak of the curves-

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:33.140
-we're trying to flatten right now, but this still doesn't explain-

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:37.560
-why most people are surprised by how fast this pandemic is spreading.

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:38.860
So here's a final example:

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:42.200
The Grand Canyon is the largest canyon on the planet.

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:47.680
It's 277 miles long and has a volume of 1000 cubic miles.

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:50.440
You could pour every single river on earth-

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:53.980
-into the Grand Canyon and still only fill half of it.

00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:58.400
A water droplet, on the other hand contains 0,05 ml-

00:03:58.460 --> 00:04:02.360
-of water. A miniscule amount. But in this example, every minute that goes by,

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:04.860
this droplet doubles in size.

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:06.280
2 drops, 4 drops, 8...

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:12.100
I'm sure you've caught on by now that it'll eventually fill up the entire Canyon and you'd be right, but how long will that take?

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:13.740
Imagine this:

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:16.080
You're finishing up a hike down in the Grand Canyon, and-

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:21.420
-you get word that 20 minutes ago a magic drop of water started doubling its size every single minute.

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:22.320
That's weird.

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:25.340
Even your Grandpa's never heard of that happening in his entire life!

00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:30.470
But in the 20 minutes since it started, it's only gotten to the size of a couple jugs of water. Um,

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:31.960
okay.

00:04:31.960 --> 00:04:33.640
You shrug it and move on with your hike.

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:36.860
Another 15 minutes go by when you get a warning alert on your phone.

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:41.260
The park is now in emergency lockdown and everyone has to evacuate immediately.

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:45.120
The water has now reached the size of an Olympic swimming pool.

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:46.880
You are already heading back to your car,

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:48.480
but this is ridiculous!

00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:50.740
What kind of a danger does a swimming pool represent?

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:53.000
Have they forgotten that you're in the Grand Canyon?!

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:57.660
Sure. I mean if you hurried you can get out quicker, but it's a nice day out and what about your freedom?

00:04:57.750 --> 00:05:03.170
Finally an hour has gone by since the water droplet began to double and you finally notice it.

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:04.850
You're nearing the top of the canyon,

00:05:04.850 --> 00:05:07.380
but you can see it way down there at the bottom.

00:05:07.460 --> 00:05:12.660
At the 60 minute mark, the water droplet has now overtaken the volume of the Colorado River.

00:05:12.700 --> 00:05:14.860
Literal alarms are now blaring,

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:17.680
Everyone's panicking, people are being airlifted to safety!

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:19.600
But they don't have room for you.

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:22.980
It took an hour for the water to reach a point where you could finally see it

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:26.820
How much more time until the water fills the entire canyon.

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:27.720
The answer?

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:32.120
You've now run out of time to escape.

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:43.120
Now obviously this is an absurd scenario,

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:45.980
but you could probably see the parallels with the spread of the current pandemic.

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:49.440
Fortunately, it is not as bad as my examples might make it seem.

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:52.560
However, it is indeed proving to be a world-changing event,

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:55.400
that not enough people took seriously soon enough.

00:05:55.400 --> 00:06:00.660
Perhaps now you understand the importance of the unprecedented measures currently locking down society.

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:03.500
You see, it was never going to seem like it was that bad,

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:04.720
until it was.

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:07.760
It's really hard to say where we'll be in the near future

00:06:08.020 --> 00:06:11.640
Are we nearing an inflection point and things will start going back to normal soon?

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:15.220
Or are we sitting in a canyon watching the water level slowly rise?

00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:18.120
I wish I could answer that but I can't.

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:21.500
The only way we'll beat this, is by stepping up individually.

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:26.080
Be responsible. And even though we're all apart, we'll make it through this together.

